From 0f0829cf9d57a2d6aa6f3e9942f924109507cc0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 21:07:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] feat(vendor): skip recompositing/reblitting unchanged
 rows in Renderer

Adds an opt-in RenderDirtyMode::SkipUnchangedRows to Renderer::render():
the page rows whose render cells and lines are identical to the previous
frame's, and that the cursor neither left nor entered, are not drawn again.
Renderer::lastDirtyRows() reports the rows that were painted, sorted, so a
frontend whose surface keeps its pixels (the native macOS CPU/CoreGraphics
backend) can erase and re-blit exactly those rows. The default, FullFrame,
leaves every existing caller untouched.

Motivation: the CPU backend recomposites the whole visible page every
frame, which becomes perceptible typing lag on a large (full-screen) page.

Rebase note (v0.8.0.9335): redesigned on this rebase, from an engine
primitive (per-line revision snapshots in the RenderBuffer) plus a renderer
consumer into a renderer-only comparison, for two reasons found while
porting it. (1) The old consumer indexed its snapshot by grid LineOffset,
but RenderCell::position.line is the screen row (plus a top status line's
height), so the mapping was right only unscrolled and without a top status
line -- and Grid::render() now also takes a fold projection whose rows are
not contiguous at all. (2) A line's revision tracks its content only: the
selection, search and link highlights, a palette change, reverse video and
blinking all change a row's pixels without touching its revision, and would
have left stale rows behind. Comparing the render buffer's own rows --
everything the builder resolved into them -- sees all of those. The
cursor's rows (and the rows a cursor motion sweeps) and blinking rows are
redrawn regardless; a geometry or default-background change, a cleared
atlas or an applied reconfiguration repaints everything; and so does a
frame carrying what 0.8.0 draws outside the page rows (status bar, gutter,
annotations, sticky header, line flash), none of which the native frontend
enables. Upstream's RenderBuffer and Terminal are no longer touched by this
patch. The frontend consumer is still to be written.

